# Orkeon {{VERSION}} — {{RID}}

Command-line executables for the [Orkeon](https://github.com/Orkeon/orkeon) AI agent framework.

## Prerequisite

**If this is the CLI archive** (`bin/` holds `orkeon` and, on Windows and Linux, the
Orkeon Studio apps): no .NET. Everything in it is self-contained — the .NET runtime
ships inside.

**If `bin/` holds every launcher** (the multi-app archive): `orkeon`,
`orkeon-host` and the Orkeon Studio apps are self-contained;
`orkeon-slim` and `orkeon-repl` are **framework-dependent** and
require the **.NET 10 runtime** (`Microsoft.NETCore.App 10.x`). Install it from
<https://dotnet.microsoft.com/download/dotnet/10.0>, then verify with
`dotnet --list-runtimes`. The installers below check for it: `install.sh` prints
the install commands for your platform if it is missing, `install.ps1` prints a
warning with the download link above — neither installs the runtime for you.
Devs who already have .NET 10 can use the smaller `orkeon-slim` instead of
`orkeon`.

## Install

Linux / macOS:

```sh
./install.sh                 # installs to ~/.local (add --prefix /usr/local for system-wide)
./install.sh --modify-path   # also appends ~/.local/bin to your shell rc files
./install.sh --uninstall
```

Windows — double-click `install.cmd`, or from a terminal (PowerShell or `cmd`):

```powershell
.\install.cmd                # installs to %LOCALAPPDATA%\Programs\Orkeon, updates user PATH
.\install.cmd -Uninstall
```

`install.cmd` starts `install.ps1` on the PowerShell that ships with Windows, under an
execution policy that holds for that one command: no PowerShell 7 to install, no
`Set-ExecutionPolicy`, no setting of the machine changed. Its arguments go through
(`-InstallDir <folder>`, `-Uninstall`). `install.ps1` itself is written for Windows
PowerShell 5.1 and for PowerShell 7, should you rather run it yourself.

Running it again over an installation replaces it. Close Orkeon Studio and let a running
crew finish first: while a program of the installation is running, the installer refuses,
names it, and leaves the installation as it was.

If the archive was downloaded by a browser, Windows may ask you to confirm before it runs
`install.cmd`. A policy your organization sets (Group Policy) overrules the launcher:
only a signed script runs there — ask your administrator.

## First run

In a **new** terminal (so the PATH change is picked up):

```sh
orkeon init      # pick an LLM provider and model; writes the global per-user config
orkeon doctor    # checks the install: runtime, config, LLM reachability, esbuild, grammars, ...
orkeon run crew.yaml
```

Configuration lives at `%APPDATA%\Orkeon\appsettings.json` (Windows) or
`~/.config/Orkeon/appsettings.json` (Linux/macOS) — never in this install
directory, which every (re)install deletes. Run with no `Llm` section configured
and Orkeon warns on stderr, then falls back to an echo provider that replays your
prompt instead of answering it: that warning means `orkeon init` has not run yet.

## Contents

The CLI archive (`orkeon-cli-{{VERSION}}-{{RID}}`) ships `bin/orkeon` — the first row
below — and the Orkeon Studio apps of its platform: `orkeon-studio` (the desktop app) on
Windows, `orkeon-studio-config` and `orkeon-studio-run` (the terminal apps) on Linux,
none on macOS. The multi-app archive ships every command in this table, and the Studio
apps too.

| Command | Description |
|---|---|
| `orkeon` | **The entry point** — runs everything the retired `orkeon-examples` did and more. `orkeon run crew.yaml` executes the YAML crews under `examples/`; `orkeon run script.ork.ts` runs the `.ork.ts` scripting DSL. Self-contained (no .NET runtime needed). |
| `orkeon-slim` | Same CLI as `orkeon`, framework-dependent (smaller; requires the .NET 10 runtime). Pick this if you already have .NET 10. |
| `orkeon-repl` | Full interactive REPL console (all built-in tools, code analysis, local embeddings) |
| `orkeon-host` | The crew service host daemon. Register it with your service manager rather than running it from a shell — see `deploy/` in this archive and `docs/architecture/service-host.md` (the `bin` wrapper exists for terminal diagnostics only). Self-contained. |

## Layout & esbuild

```
bin/                     thin launchers (the only directory that goes on your PATH)
libexec/                 one self-contained publish per application
libexec/esbuild-bin/esbuild   shared esbuild binary used to transpile .ork.ts scripts
deploy/                  service-host deployment assets (systemd unit, SCM registration script) — multi-app archive only
VERSION                  plain-text version string (drives the Add/Remove Programs entry)
INSTALL-CHANNEL          one word: the channel this tree is installed through, which `orkeon doctor` names
appsettings.sample.json  reference-only config sample, see "First run" above
LICENSE.md               Orkeon's license (MIT)
THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.md   the notices of the third-party components this archive redistributes
licenses/                the license and third-party notices of the bundled .NET runtime, one folder per runtime pack
```

The `bin/` launchers set `ORKEON_ESBUILD_PATH` to the bundled esbuild
automatically (unless you already set it yourself). If you invoke an apphost
directly from `libexec/`, either export `ORKEON_ESBUILD_PATH`, or have
`esbuild` available on your PATH.

